1
00:00:02,282 --> 00:00:10,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, I was thinking about faith that's encouraged to be developed in this training.

2
00:00:12,106 --> 00:00:17,628
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a... one should train in developing faith, basically.

3
00:00:18,328 --> 00:00:29,392
[SPEAKER_01]: But it comes in that context of five powers, you know, faith, moral shame, moral dread, energy,

4
00:00:31,319 --> 00:00:32,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And wisdom.

5
00:00:32,880 --> 00:00:33,200
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

6
00:00:34,621 --> 00:00:35,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Five powers.

7
00:00:35,722 --> 00:00:39,424
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it is, isn't it?

8
00:00:39,444 --> 00:00:39,765
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

9
00:00:42,627 --> 00:00:47,510
[SPEAKER_01]: And faith in... Like, it's really, we all have faith.

10
00:00:52,133 --> 00:00:55,155
[SPEAKER_01]: We have faith in the Buddha.

11
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:01,582
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, through reasoning, it's a good teaching, it makes sense.

12
00:01:02,463 --> 00:01:02,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

13
00:01:04,684 --> 00:01:07,386
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you decide to walk the path.

14
00:01:08,847 --> 00:01:18,193
[SPEAKER_01]: Because everyone has faith in some form or another.

15
00:01:18,733 --> 00:01:20,574
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, in regard to something.

16
00:01:21,155 --> 00:01:21,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith in the world.

17
00:01:23,256 --> 00:01:23,516
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

18
00:01:23,536 --> 00:01:26,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith in education, faith in the family.

19
00:01:29,508 --> 00:01:36,334
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you think that... you believe in pham, you believe in sensuality, you say.

20
00:01:37,074 --> 00:01:43,720
[SPEAKER_01]: You think it's gonna do you good, so you place your faith in it.

21
00:01:45,082 --> 00:01:50,727
[SPEAKER_01]: But somehow, you don't really recognize that we're placing faith in these things.

22
00:01:53,051 --> 00:01:53,511
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, sure.

23
00:01:53,752 --> 00:02:00,796
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, a person who doesn't develop self-awareness on account of their own faith, which is... the faith is inevitable.

24
00:02:00,916 --> 00:02:03,018
[SPEAKER_00]: You have to have faith in something or believe in something.

25
00:02:03,838 --> 00:02:08,341
[SPEAKER_00]: When you say, I don't believe in anything, that's your belief, basically.

26
00:02:09,022 --> 00:02:12,664
[SPEAKER_00]: But whether a person recognizes that or not...

27
00:02:12,684 --> 00:02:14,605
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, if you recognize that... Yeah.

28
00:02:15,846 --> 00:02:17,107
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the...

29
00:02:18,297 --> 00:02:20,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, then you start feeling responsibility for your faith.

30
00:02:21,339 --> 00:02:23,920
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you can't be just justified, well, this is my belief.

31
00:02:24,741 --> 00:02:26,962
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you realize you choose to hold that belief.

32
00:02:27,522 --> 00:02:30,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're responsible, which means you can also choose to stop holding it.

33
00:02:31,465 --> 00:02:33,826
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you start to scrutinize your own beliefs.

34
00:02:35,307 --> 00:02:39,829
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you might actually arrive at a better belief and better truth.

35
00:02:40,230 --> 00:02:42,311
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, more honest, more wholesome.

36
00:02:46,546 --> 00:02:48,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the power of faith.

37
00:02:49,007 --> 00:02:59,031
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, the power of faith would be, say, somebody has fully scrutinized it, is fully accepted responsibility for it, and then he realizes what matters.

38
00:02:59,871 --> 00:03:01,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from suffering matters.

39
00:03:01,752 --> 00:03:05,333
[SPEAKER_00]: That's pretty much your... Whatever people do in their life, it's because of how they feel.

40
00:03:06,017 --> 00:03:14,702
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you realize, why don't I have faith, then, in something that actually pertains to this, like, absolute, fundamental, most crucial points of human existence?

41
00:03:15,242 --> 00:03:18,124
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like a random faith in this or that, which is sort of perishable.

42
00:03:19,785 --> 00:03:28,950
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, that faith becomes his power, because, because of that faith, he then starts to apply himself according to that instruction or teaching and so on.

43
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:34,053
[SPEAKER_00]: The similarly, the, the, like, the, the, the, the faith in a doctor you would have.

44
00:03:35,728 --> 00:03:42,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless you start scrutinizing, you're just going to put your faith in the first quack that comes from... comes your way, basically.

45
00:03:44,074 --> 00:03:48,718
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... you just won't... won't think further, because it's just, ah, well, you know, it's a doctor.

46
00:03:48,958 --> 00:03:50,159
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't need to know these things.

47
00:03:50,279 --> 00:03:50,880
[SPEAKER_00]: He'll help me.

48
00:03:51,380 --> 00:03:54,742
[SPEAKER_00]: But, he might want to help you, but he might not be able to.

49
00:03:54,983 --> 00:04:00,547
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, he himself is unaware that he's not actually adequate doctor.

50
00:04:00,587 --> 00:04:04,530
[SPEAKER_00]: So, unless you start scrutinizing it as well, you're at a lot of risk.

51
00:04:06,055 --> 00:04:09,196
[SPEAKER_00]: But you start scrutinizing, and then you place that faith correctly.

52
00:04:09,316 --> 00:04:10,296
[SPEAKER_00]: Somebody is like, no, no.

53
00:04:10,736 --> 00:04:12,817
[SPEAKER_00]: This guy can cure me from this, to this, to this.

54
00:04:13,097 --> 00:04:16,038
[SPEAKER_00]: He can manage this symptom, or that symptom, or some other symptom.

55
00:04:16,538 --> 00:04:20,019
[SPEAKER_00]: But this doctor uproots the actual illness.

56
00:04:20,839 --> 00:04:21,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Or so it seems.

57
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:23,560
[SPEAKER_00]: I need to find out.

58
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:25,420
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in that sense, you develop faith.

59
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:30,142
[SPEAKER_00]: And that faith is a power, if it's placed rightly.

60
00:04:30,162 --> 00:04:32,682
[SPEAKER_00]: Because without that faith, you wouldn't have started practice.

61
00:04:36,031 --> 00:04:50,183
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the same, like I said, like an Ārya... Ārya Saukha, the noble disciple, if he lacks faith, as in, could use a bit more faith in certain... certain aspects, that's why he's not free from sensuality yet.

62
00:04:52,746 --> 00:05:00,012
[SPEAKER_00]: But by developing faith, as in just reminding himself that he just needs to stick to the principle, and even the sensuality will fade.

63
00:05:00,853 --> 00:05:02,755
[SPEAKER_00]: The distraction of senses will go away.

64
00:05:04,003 --> 00:05:12,211
[SPEAKER_00]: But by not having that faith, by not reminding yourself of just sticking to the principles, eh... those things still linger.

65
00:05:15,514 --> 00:05:23,341
[SPEAKER_01]: This is... with lack of faith, a person will disrobe... a monk will disrobe... lack of faith.

66
00:05:24,022 --> 00:05:24,422
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah.

67
00:05:25,083 --> 00:05:25,443
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course.

68
00:05:26,224 --> 00:05:28,887
[SPEAKER_01]: And some... some stay monks...

69
00:05:30,112 --> 00:05:34,115
[SPEAKER_01]: They have faith, but they live their life with tears in their eyes.

70
00:05:34,516 --> 00:05:34,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Painful.

71
00:05:35,336 --> 00:05:35,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

72
00:05:35,957 --> 00:05:36,778
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no result.

73
00:05:37,258 --> 00:05:37,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

74
00:05:37,919 --> 00:05:39,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they have faith in wholesome qualities.

75
00:05:40,781 --> 00:05:41,702
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there's no result.

76
00:05:42,182 --> 00:05:42,783
[SPEAKER_01]: But they see.

77
00:05:43,383 --> 00:05:43,964
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

78
00:05:43,984 --> 00:05:45,865
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, that's better.

79
00:05:46,886 --> 00:05:48,307
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still better than disrobing, yeah.

80
00:05:48,567 --> 00:05:48,848
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

81
00:05:49,508 --> 00:05:49,828
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

82
00:05:50,689 --> 00:05:55,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, even if the monk spends every single day of his life in tears of restraint and stuff, it's still better than disrobing.

83
00:05:56,054 --> 00:05:56,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

84
00:05:57,460 --> 00:06:00,141
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's still actually making effort.

85
00:06:00,642 --> 00:06:01,242
[SPEAKER_00]: That counts.

86
00:06:03,623 --> 00:06:05,804
[SPEAKER_01]: But also, the faith is inevitable.

87
00:06:06,044 --> 00:06:08,105
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what are you going to place your faith in?

88
00:06:09,126 --> 00:06:09,646
[SPEAKER_01]: In something.

89
00:06:10,046 --> 00:06:13,068
[SPEAKER_01]: Either the world or the dhamma.

90
00:06:13,088 --> 00:06:14,548
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the renunciation world.

91
00:06:14,588 --> 00:06:15,489
[SPEAKER_00]: Renunciation, yeah.

92
00:06:15,529 --> 00:06:16,089
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is the dhamma.

93
00:06:16,109 --> 00:06:16,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Beyond the world.

94
00:06:16,489 --> 00:06:16,689
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

95
00:06:17,170 --> 00:06:17,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

96
00:06:18,642 --> 00:06:19,703
[SPEAKER_00]: No, that's what I mean.

97
00:06:20,063 --> 00:06:23,106
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not really concerned with this particular faith or that.

98
00:06:23,126 --> 00:06:39,362
[SPEAKER_00]: You start looking at the fundamental aspects, and it exactly comes down to you either want the world, have faith in it, as something that's friendly, disposed towards you, or you don't have that faith.

99
00:06:40,683 --> 00:06:42,605
[SPEAKER_00]: You have faith in actually renouncing the world.

100
00:06:47,599 --> 00:06:48,719
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't have it both ways.

101
00:06:50,140 --> 00:06:56,463
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't say, well, I'm renouncing the world, but I also think the world is quite... quite friendly towards me.

102
00:06:56,943 --> 00:07:00,224
[SPEAKER_00]: Friendly disposition, safe place, all these things.

103
00:07:00,284 --> 00:07:01,024
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you wouldn't.

104
00:07:01,064 --> 00:07:04,186
[SPEAKER_00]: It's either you accept it, or you want to overcome it.

105
00:07:05,946 --> 00:07:17,151
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as we said, it's better to, even if, like, you're not necessarily arriving at any results, to still maintain the faith in renunciation, faith in overcoming the world, faith in saying no to the world.

106
00:07:21,805 --> 00:07:25,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is a chance that wisdom can arise out of it.

107
00:07:25,495 --> 00:07:29,986
[SPEAKER_00]: But certainly, wisdom won't arise if you said yes to the world and went back to the senses.

108
00:07:35,288 --> 00:07:36,808
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then it's difficult.

109
00:07:36,828 --> 00:07:38,409
[SPEAKER_01]: It's through reasoning you have faith.

110
00:07:38,649 --> 00:07:41,169
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, if there's no results... Sure.

111
00:07:41,189 --> 00:07:44,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Then... Well, that's why monks disrobe.

112
00:07:44,390 --> 00:07:48,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, mind you, many of them disrobe because they never really clarify the reasons for the practice to begin with.

113
00:07:49,371 --> 00:07:54,993
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, just... while the inspiration lasted... And that's another thing, actually.

114
00:07:55,193 --> 00:07:55,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

115
00:07:55,813 --> 00:07:56,913
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it's good to point out.

116
00:07:57,533 --> 00:07:59,574
[SPEAKER_00]: People confuse faith and inspiration as well.

117
00:08:00,474 --> 00:08:02,394
[SPEAKER_00]: They think when they feel inspired, they have faith.

118
00:08:02,834 --> 00:08:03,975
[SPEAKER_00]: It has nothing to do with faith.

119
00:08:05,815 --> 00:08:09,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith is that recognition of, this will be beneficial for me.

120
00:08:09,819 --> 00:08:12,241
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know for sure, but it seems so.

121
00:08:12,922 --> 00:08:16,125
[SPEAKER_00]: So, because of that recognition, you apply yourself.

122
00:08:17,006 --> 00:08:24,793
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the Buddha points out when he says, even if it hurts, even if there are no results, you keep doing it.

123
00:08:25,434 --> 00:08:27,476
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it comes out of a recognition of wholesome.

124
00:08:29,338 --> 00:08:32,960
[SPEAKER_00]: But inspiration, inspiration, it's like when you're riding the wave.

125
00:08:33,161 --> 00:08:35,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, everything is easy because you're so inspired.

126
00:08:35,562 --> 00:08:38,684
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like a spiritual honeymoon.

127
00:08:39,004 --> 00:08:40,645
[SPEAKER_00]: But that will have to run out.

128
00:08:41,406 --> 00:08:45,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And you would have been better off not even giving rise to it, but some people can't help it.

129
00:08:46,228 --> 00:08:49,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But once it runs its course, that's where the practice starts.

130
00:08:50,271 --> 00:08:51,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Not like when you feel like doing it.

131
00:08:52,072 --> 00:08:53,092
[SPEAKER_00]: It's when you don't want to do it.

132
00:08:54,093 --> 00:08:55,494
[SPEAKER_00]: And you want to go back to the world.

133
00:09:00,591 --> 00:09:14,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So, basically, faith... Inspiration would be intoxication, a form of intoxication of your faith.

134
00:09:15,301 --> 00:09:19,484
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, there is a prospect of escape, freedom from suffering, so you get so inspired.

135
00:09:22,226 --> 00:09:23,387
[SPEAKER_00]: In itself, that's not a problem.

136
00:09:23,427 --> 00:09:25,889
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that becomes your

137
00:09:27,485 --> 00:09:34,350
[SPEAKER_00]: Main kind of... where your efforts are rooted in, then you will be in danger.

138
00:09:34,370 --> 00:09:36,471
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that intoxication will fail.

139
00:09:36,531 --> 00:09:41,275
[SPEAKER_00]: That energy that you got from that initial inspiration will have to subside.

140
00:09:42,556 --> 00:09:46,999
[SPEAKER_00]: But if that was the sole ground for your efforts, then your efforts will go with it.

141
00:09:48,620 --> 00:09:51,181
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's where the reflexive reasoning beforehand.

142
00:09:51,262 --> 00:09:51,802
[SPEAKER_00]: Actually, no.

143
00:09:52,724 --> 00:09:53,924
[SPEAKER_00]: I might be inspired or not.

144
00:09:53,964 --> 00:09:54,925
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm inspired, great.

145
00:09:54,945 --> 00:09:56,385
[SPEAKER_00]: If I'm not, well, that's also great.

146
00:09:56,965 --> 00:09:59,426
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to keep doing it.

147
00:10:03,027 --> 00:10:04,247
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is to keep doing it.

148
00:10:05,548 --> 00:10:07,528
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the...

149
00:10:09,689 --> 00:10:19,012
[SPEAKER_01]: The thing with this faith power, it arises, it seems to suggest, with the other four powers.

150
00:10:21,155 --> 00:10:25,177
[SPEAKER_01]: It seems to indicate what... those other four things indicate what the faith is about.

151
00:10:25,917 --> 00:10:27,118
[SPEAKER_01]: It has already got wisdom.

152
00:10:27,958 --> 00:10:29,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already consisting of energy.

153
00:10:29,399 --> 00:10:30,219
[SPEAKER_00]: It contains, yeah.

154
00:10:30,699 --> 00:10:33,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It contains the possibility of it, yeah.

155
00:10:35,341 --> 00:10:44,305
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if your faith is just based on, I don't know, the appearance of somebody who's talking about some teaching or something,

156
00:10:45,687 --> 00:10:50,008
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, or the charisma, or the, the energy, in verse comma.

157
00:10:50,988 --> 00:10:52,129
[SPEAKER_00]: Man, that, that's not faith.

158
00:10:52,169 --> 00:10:53,389
[SPEAKER_00]: That's just intoxication.

159
00:10:53,629 --> 00:10:54,369
[SPEAKER_00]: That's laziness.

160
00:10:55,270 --> 00:11:01,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Where you don't want to do the work of scrutinizing, but you just want to ride off the whale and spray, oh, he must be an arahant.

161
00:11:01,971 --> 00:11:04,572
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's go and live with him, as if that does anything to you.

162
00:11:05,993 --> 00:11:10,074
[SPEAKER_00]: The only way you'll benefit from an arahant is if you start understanding what being an arahant is.

163
00:11:11,794 --> 00:11:14,595
[SPEAKER_00]: But, and also, how can Bhutujana know who an arahant is?

164
00:11:16,148 --> 00:11:17,589
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you know it must be an arahant?

165
00:11:17,709 --> 00:11:18,669
[SPEAKER_00]: What difference does it make?

166
00:11:19,969 --> 00:11:25,151
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if your faith is not rooted in that blind intoxication, then you'll be scrutinizing.

167
00:11:25,972 --> 00:11:27,292
[SPEAKER_00]: How does that person live?

168
00:11:29,133 --> 00:11:29,953
[SPEAKER_00]: What do they teach?

169
00:11:31,014 --> 00:11:32,174
[SPEAKER_00]: Do they live what they teach?

170
00:11:33,034 --> 00:11:34,175
[SPEAKER_00]: Does their teaching make sense?

171
00:11:34,395 --> 00:11:39,217
[SPEAKER_00]: Does their teaching pertain to the ultimate goal that results in liberation?

172
00:11:39,437 --> 00:11:44,919
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start seeing yes, or if you're not sure, okay, come closer, have a closer look, ask more questions to yourself.

173
00:11:45,983 --> 00:11:50,324
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if it seems more right than other things you've encountered, then you have to start doing it.

174
00:11:50,845 --> 00:11:53,485
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't just keep your distance and expect to understand it.

175
00:11:54,386 --> 00:11:55,666
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where the faith comes in.

176
00:11:55,946 --> 00:11:59,747
[SPEAKER_00]: Once you scrutinize, okay, now, I'll start with this, then we'll see.

177
00:12:00,447 --> 00:12:08,450
[SPEAKER_00]: You still don't commit to it blindly, you keep reflecting on what you're doing, but you stop looking for faults as well.

178
00:12:11,794 --> 00:12:14,016
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you start thinking like that from the start, then, yeah, exactly.

179
00:12:14,876 --> 00:12:23,202
[SPEAKER_00]: If that was... if your efforts are correct, if your scrutinizing has reached a sufficient amount, you will start recognizing certain criteria, and then the wisdom will be arising from it.

180
00:12:24,563 --> 00:12:32,048
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the energy will be arising from wisdom, the whole, like... well, the verification from fear, from suffering will be arising from wisdom, from understanding.

181
00:12:33,069 --> 00:12:38,373
[SPEAKER_01]: You can build up a momentum from that, eh... Yeah, in a way, yeah.

182
00:12:39,325 --> 00:12:41,687
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's not a momentum of inspiration, as in...

183
00:12:43,729 --> 00:12:45,350
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, it's obvious how I'm using inspiration.

184
00:12:45,410 --> 00:12:50,235
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if other people use word inspiration for that manner, that's fine, but I, I, I qualify it.

185
00:12:50,915 --> 00:12:51,115
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

186
00:12:51,516 --> 00:12:56,900
[SPEAKER_00]: Five minutes ago, it's usually inspiration means, you know, it's kind of taking you, not you taking it.

187
00:12:59,783 --> 00:13:05,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, faith, the inspiration takes, takes you, you don't, but faith you take it.

188
00:13:05,825 --> 00:13:06,425
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

189
00:13:06,845 --> 00:13:10,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Inspiration is when faith... when you make the faith take you, basically.

190
00:13:13,327 --> 00:13:16,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Guru says you must have faith in wholesome qualities.

191
00:13:16,367 --> 00:13:18,248
[SPEAKER_01]: He has faith in wholesome qualities.

192
00:13:18,488 --> 00:13:21,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he read these are wholesome, why they're wholesome.

193
00:13:22,069 --> 00:13:22,709
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a safety.

194
00:13:23,709 --> 00:13:24,929
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's exactly the thing.

195
00:13:25,049 --> 00:13:32,551
[SPEAKER_00]: Like somebody who starts, they don't know why is wholesome wholesome and why is unwholesome unwholesome.

196
00:13:34,655 --> 00:13:35,755
[SPEAKER_00]: But they need to have faith in it.

197
00:13:36,496 --> 00:13:38,656
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you take on the virtue and the sīla.

198
00:13:40,397 --> 00:13:40,857
[SPEAKER_00]: On trust.

199
00:13:42,738 --> 00:13:46,499
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have faith because, on some level, it's kind of making a bit of sense.

200
00:13:47,819 --> 00:13:50,280
[SPEAKER_00]: That restraint is better than non-restraint.

201
00:13:52,621 --> 00:14:01,984
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of that faith, because of adhering to that principle that you just turn through the faith, then, then actually you get to see for yourself wholesome is wholesome, unwholesome is unwholesome.

202
00:14:02,498 --> 00:14:14,692
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when Sotāpanna's faith... sorry, when Pratyekabuddha becomes Sotāpanna, his previous faith becomes confirmed confidence in the dhamma, in the wholesome as wholesome, in that criteria.

203
00:14:14,752 --> 00:14:16,014
[SPEAKER_00]: He sees it for himself now.

204
00:14:16,514 --> 00:14:19,398
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, for him, faith becomes inconceivable.

205
00:14:19,638 --> 00:14:22,862
[SPEAKER_00]: That faith is gone, because now he's confident about it.

206
00:14:26,858 --> 00:14:32,100
[SPEAKER_00]: A degree of ignorance, as in you don't know it to the extent you don't know it, to that extent you have faith in it.

207
00:14:32,440 --> 00:14:37,082
[SPEAKER_00]: But not like faith, as I said, oh, I have faith that this will sort all my problems out.

208
00:14:37,682 --> 00:14:41,903
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I have faith that if I do this, I will sort all my problems out.

209
00:14:43,844 --> 00:14:54,908
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a, like a, not even so subtle shift, but many people would sort of fail to, eh... Like faith in a meditation technique, for example.

210
00:14:55,860 --> 00:15:11,611
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, people can invest lots of effort and stuff, but the faith is placed on the content of what they're doing, as in, if I keep doing this, then the solution of all the problems, as in the enlightenment, freedom from suffering, will happen to me, because I've been adhering to this.

211
00:15:12,692 --> 00:15:21,298
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's completely blind faith, because they're completely divorced from any rational recognition of, well, wait a minute, what is...

212
00:15:23,290 --> 00:15:26,232
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it wholesome to just be doing this meditation technique?

213
00:15:27,632 --> 00:15:28,773
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I understanding anything?

214
00:15:28,793 --> 00:15:29,793
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

215
00:15:29,853 --> 00:15:33,455
[SPEAKER_00]: What are you going after when you're doing a random observance?

216
00:15:33,475 --> 00:15:34,835
[SPEAKER_00]: You're going after experiences.

217
00:15:35,235 --> 00:15:39,477
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have faith that random experiences, one of them will be the right experience.

218
00:15:40,178 --> 00:15:44,079
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you have faith that a random experience will do the work for you.

219
00:15:45,240 --> 00:15:46,941
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, will result in enlightenment.

220
00:15:48,321 --> 00:15:48,521
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

221
00:15:48,821 --> 00:15:49,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Adhering to wholesome

222
00:15:51,129 --> 00:15:52,250
[SPEAKER_00]: Avoiding unwholesome.

223
00:15:52,630 --> 00:15:54,051
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting upon wholesome.

224
00:15:54,311 --> 00:15:55,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Reflecting upon unwholesome.

225
00:15:56,452 --> 00:15:57,533
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping the precepts.

226
00:15:57,693 --> 00:15:58,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Having a sense of shame.

227
00:15:58,833 --> 00:15:59,814
[SPEAKER_00]: Having a sense of shame.

228
00:16:00,034 --> 00:16:00,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

229
00:16:01,555 --> 00:16:03,316
[SPEAKER_00]: And there is no technique that can... Making an effort.

230
00:16:03,336 --> 00:16:04,056
[SPEAKER_00]: ...that can encompass it.

231
00:16:04,096 --> 00:16:05,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Making an effort, yeah.

232
00:16:06,018 --> 00:16:08,459
[SPEAKER_01]: No one... no technique will make the effort for you.

233
00:16:08,759 --> 00:16:10,640
[SPEAKER_01]: No technique will give you the wisdom.

234
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:11,241
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

235
00:16:11,721 --> 00:16:13,902
[SPEAKER_01]: No technique will make you have a sense of shame.

236
00:16:14,402 --> 00:16:14,803
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

237
00:16:15,023 --> 00:16:18,605
[SPEAKER_00]: Or fear... People think, well, all these things are necessary.

238
00:16:19,425 --> 00:16:21,466
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, the technique will get you to that point.

239
00:16:22,567 --> 00:16:26,610
[SPEAKER_00]: But the faith in the point, that's the blind faith I talk about.

240
00:16:27,891 --> 00:16:28,952
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a mistaken faith.

241
00:16:29,932 --> 00:16:36,437
[SPEAKER_00]: The faith should be in your effort, not to the point that randomly arises, sometimes does, sometimes doesn't.

242
00:16:37,418 --> 00:16:40,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Some novelty experience, that is completely secondary.

243
00:16:42,730 --> 00:16:43,671
[SPEAKER_00]: The faith is in that.

244
00:16:43,751 --> 00:16:46,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandon... Has faith in abandoning unwholesome?

245
00:16:46,914 --> 00:16:48,635
[SPEAKER_00]: Has faith in cultivating wholesome?

246
00:16:49,035 --> 00:16:52,598
[SPEAKER_00]: How can you cultivate something or abandon something if you're not discerning what that thing is?

247
00:16:53,439 --> 00:16:56,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And how can you discern it if you never make the effort to think about it?

248
00:16:57,382 --> 00:17:07,491
[SPEAKER_00]: If all of your efforts are aimed at suppressing your thinking and focusing on one point and whatnot, to get the energy releases and so on, and then I'll see I had faith and I had this energy release, I must be a sūtra.

249
00:17:19,366 --> 00:17:30,170
[SPEAKER_00]: All those things occur bodily, in the body, for you, and have nothing to do with understanding the nature of it.

250
00:17:32,631 --> 00:17:33,612
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you should have faith in...

251
00:17:34,012 --> 00:17:36,773
[SPEAKER_00]: I have faith in the possibility of understanding the nature of things.

252
00:17:37,693 --> 00:17:44,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that I'm going to have faith in this particular method of walking or breathing or something, whereby I won't have to think about anything else.

253
00:17:45,939 --> 00:17:48,422
[SPEAKER_00]: If I just adhere to it, it will sort me out.

254
00:17:50,063 --> 00:17:51,344
[SPEAKER_01]: While not in the Buddha's teaching.

255
00:17:53,807 --> 00:17:54,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, having faith in the Buddha.

256
00:18:03,824 --> 00:18:04,525
[SPEAKER_01]: His own effort.

257
00:18:04,905 --> 00:18:05,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

258
00:18:05,626 --> 00:18:11,351
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't follow so-and-so's technique, which bing, bing, and then all of a sudden led him to enlightenment.

259
00:18:11,411 --> 00:18:15,274
[SPEAKER_00]: And when they asked him how, he described the understanding of the suffering and fear from it.

260
00:18:15,294 --> 00:18:25,123
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't say, oh, well, by walking three steps to the left, four steps to the right, by watching your nostrils and observing the movements of your belly, or sensations in your shoulders and whatnot.

261
00:18:25,263 --> 00:18:25,443
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

262
00:18:26,024 --> 00:18:30,768
[SPEAKER_00]: He said, by understanding suffering, cause of suffering,

263
00:18:32,342 --> 00:18:36,325
[SPEAKER_00]: cessation of suffering and the way it leads to the cessation of suffering.

264
00:18:37,325 --> 00:18:38,426
[SPEAKER_00]: A full understanding.

265
00:18:40,107 --> 00:18:41,568
[SPEAKER_00]: A full understanding is exactly that.

266
00:18:41,608 --> 00:18:42,169
[SPEAKER_00]: You understood it.

267
00:18:42,489 --> 00:18:48,133
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, like, knowingly, for him, knowingly, those things arose, knowingly, knowingly, knowingly, those things ceased.

268
00:18:48,812 --> 00:18:50,733
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, he knew, he understood it through and through.

269
00:18:50,753 --> 00:18:52,294
[SPEAKER_00]: It wasn't like, oh, this is wrong.

270
00:18:52,514 --> 00:18:52,934
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it?

271
00:18:53,014 --> 00:18:55,355
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it must be this experience of freedom from suffering.

272
00:18:55,836 --> 00:18:56,936
[SPEAKER_00]: Means there's no understanding there.

273
00:18:56,956 --> 00:18:57,897
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just a guesswork.

274
00:18:57,957 --> 00:19:00,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Just mystifying now, and hoping that it's that.

275
00:19:00,838 --> 00:19:04,440
[SPEAKER_00]: But the fact that you don't know that it is, means it isn't.

276
00:19:05,781 --> 00:19:07,762
[SPEAKER_00]: Because freedom from suffering comes through the knowledge of it.

277
00:19:07,902 --> 00:19:10,663
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows that he knows, as the Buddha would describe, sotāpanna.

278
00:19:12,064 --> 00:19:14,285
[SPEAKER_00]: He knows that he knows freedom from suffering.

279
00:19:15,157 --> 00:19:23,705
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just he had experience and then he kind of deduced that he must be understanding freedom from suffering because, you know, I suffer less.

280
00:19:24,346 --> 00:19:26,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering less doesn't mean you're free from the nature of suffering.

281
00:19:27,048 --> 00:19:30,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffer less means that you suffer less due to circumstances or whatever.

282
00:19:31,492 --> 00:19:33,334
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means then, you're gonna suffer more.

283
00:19:34,015 --> 00:19:38,219
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you think that suffering less matters, as a measure,

284
00:19:40,024 --> 00:19:42,906
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you suffer less amount, but it's still the suffering.

285
00:19:42,946 --> 00:19:50,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, all the deva realms, all the deva realms... That, and also, if you have that view, means, you're not going to make the work, make the effort.

286
00:19:51,711 --> 00:20:00,496
[SPEAKER_00]: The, eh... No, I was going to say, if that's the view, means, and if you think that, oh, I suffer less, thus, it's good, my practice is bearing fruit.

287
00:20:00,616 --> 00:20:04,358
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see any other distinction of full understanding, but I suffer less.

288
00:20:06,423 --> 00:20:11,667
[SPEAKER_00]: And fail to see that it's because of circumstances, because of the restraint and so on, not because of wisdom.

289
00:20:11,708 --> 00:20:18,753
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when circumstances change, and they will, when the suffering hits you back, it's going to be too late to do anything about it.

290
00:20:20,395 --> 00:20:25,219
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, if you suffer less on account of your restraint and faith in the practice, that's great.

291
00:20:26,380 --> 00:20:28,562
[SPEAKER_00]: But that in itself, it's not a solution.

292
00:20:29,983 --> 00:20:31,264
[SPEAKER_00]: Suffering still touches you.

293
00:20:32,185 --> 00:20:34,707
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be not touched by the suffering in the first place.

294
00:20:37,076 --> 00:20:45,382
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, you can say you truly... not... you're not free from suffering just circumstantially, but through the understanding.

295
00:20:47,123 --> 00:21:00,073
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, if... if... if freedom from suffering is measure of... of your understanding, then, exactly, many Putugeno would sort of think... well, you... you could argue then that... then they're fine, as Putugenos, because they don't really suffer much.

296
00:21:03,050 --> 00:21:03,590
[SPEAKER_00]: But it isn't.

297
00:21:04,030 --> 00:21:08,792
[SPEAKER_00]: How much you're exposed to suffering on a particular basis, it doesn't matter.

298
00:21:09,312 --> 00:21:15,675
[SPEAKER_00]: What matters is, as the Buddha said, whether you're prey to suffering, as in whether you're liable to suffering to affect you.

299
00:21:15,955 --> 00:21:16,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Is a possibility.

300
00:21:17,315 --> 00:21:17,816
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

301
00:21:18,416 --> 00:21:21,917
[SPEAKER_00]: I might not suffer now, but is this true for the future?

302
00:21:23,158 --> 00:21:28,040
[SPEAKER_00]: If something happens to me, something unforeseen, an accident, will I be unaffected by it?

303
00:21:29,560 --> 00:21:31,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not sure, means you do not

304
00:21:42,270 --> 00:21:44,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you can truly say, yeah, it's not a problem for me.

305
00:21:46,413 --> 00:21:47,114
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will know that.

306
00:21:47,634 --> 00:21:58,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you think about, think about an accident happening to you in the future, and see, even that thought is actually contains the displeasure of that prospect, that possibility.

307
00:21:59,484 --> 00:22:02,727
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're not free from suffering, that thought will already be affecting you.

308
00:22:03,996 --> 00:22:05,217
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you are, it won't be.

309
00:22:05,557 --> 00:22:08,298
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you know, whatever comes through that thought, won't affect you either.

310
00:22:08,858 --> 00:22:10,439
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going to be an extreme inconvenience.

311
00:22:11,299 --> 00:22:12,599
[SPEAKER_00]: You can disable it or not.

312
00:22:13,400 --> 00:22:15,481
[SPEAKER_00]: But, you won't suffer on account of it.

313
00:22:21,583 --> 00:22:26,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, so, yeah, if I lose all my... all my things.

314
00:22:26,105 --> 00:22:26,385
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

315
00:22:26,605 --> 00:22:26,945
[SPEAKER_01]: Fire.

316
00:22:27,225 --> 00:22:29,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Fire, flood, thieves, yeah.

317
00:22:31,227 --> 00:22:31,447
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

318
00:22:31,607 --> 00:22:33,468
[SPEAKER_00]: Will I be affected if all this disappears?

319
00:22:37,456 --> 00:22:39,337
[SPEAKER_00]: And you don't have to go and start throwing your things away.

320
00:22:40,198 --> 00:22:43,079
[SPEAKER_00]: Just dwell on that thought, the prospect of it being taken away.

321
00:22:44,900 --> 00:22:46,601
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there emotional resistance to it?

322
00:22:47,222 --> 00:22:50,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Is there, like, a bit anxious or something, the prospect of it?

323
00:22:51,264 --> 00:22:51,844
[SPEAKER_00]: That's your work.

324
00:22:52,144 --> 00:23:00,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, not throwing away things, or accumulating, for that matter, but actually undoing that emotional connection to it.

325
00:23:01,430 --> 00:23:05,432
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that thing is not undone because you don't think about it.

326
00:23:06,052 --> 00:23:06,993
[SPEAKER_00]: That is its fuel.

327
00:23:08,911 --> 00:23:13,975
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not thinking about that emotional assumption, connection, craving.

328
00:23:15,336 --> 00:23:17,598
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you're feeding it.

329
00:23:17,618 --> 00:23:19,499
[SPEAKER_00]: If you think about it, you stop feeding it.

330
00:23:21,901 --> 00:23:30,408
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why there's all that persistent energy, all the way, all the time, constantly, until you're in arahant.

331
00:23:30,688 --> 00:23:33,950
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't have to... Because then the energy becomes your mode of being.

332
00:23:34,111 --> 00:23:35,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Your mindfulness becomes your mode of being.

333
00:23:36,922 --> 00:23:40,083
[SPEAKER_00]: And energy, effort, mindfulness becomes effortless.

334
00:23:40,223 --> 00:23:42,243
[SPEAKER_00]: All of that.

335
00:23:42,503 --> 00:23:44,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith, faith, as well.

336
00:23:44,463 --> 00:23:45,263
[SPEAKER_01]: Structures, faith.

337
00:23:45,824 --> 00:23:46,944
[SPEAKER_00]: In a way, yeah, yeah, yeah.

338
00:23:46,964 --> 00:23:50,264
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, that faith gets fully, fully converted into confidence.

339
00:23:51,385 --> 00:23:52,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith and confidence.

340
00:23:52,265 --> 00:23:53,825
[SPEAKER_00]: You verified what you had faith in.

341
00:23:54,625 --> 00:23:55,025
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

342
00:23:56,045 --> 00:23:59,466
[SPEAKER_00]: Through verifying it, you don't have faith that this works, because now you know that it works.

343
00:23:59,486 --> 00:24:00,886
[SPEAKER_00]: Trust, confidence.

344
00:24:00,906 --> 00:24:05,387
[SPEAKER_00]: So, faith, the right faith, it's like borrowing wisdom.

345
00:24:06,917 --> 00:24:08,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Borrowing the knowledge that you arrive at.

346
00:24:10,319 --> 00:24:13,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Borrowing it beforehand as something to guide you in that direction.

347
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:16,301
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you arrive at it, and that's how you pay it back.

348
00:24:17,222 --> 00:24:17,442
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

349
00:24:18,562 --> 00:24:25,725
[SPEAKER_00]: But exactly what you said, if that faith is not rooted in that wisdom, you won't be borrowing wisdom when you when you develop faith.

350
00:24:26,206 --> 00:24:27,366
[SPEAKER_00]: You're borrowing more ignorance.

351
00:24:30,948 --> 00:24:33,349
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no reason to relax in the practice.

352
00:24:33,929 --> 00:24:35,250
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no reason, there's no need.

353
00:24:39,362 --> 00:24:48,108
[SPEAKER_00]: The only time you need to relax in your practice is when you assume that pleasures of your senses is relaxation.

354
00:24:50,349 --> 00:24:58,635
[SPEAKER_00]: That's when people want to relax or distract from the practice, because they still assume that absence of effort means relaxation.

355
00:24:58,815 --> 00:24:59,356
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't.

356
00:25:03,719 --> 00:25:05,420
[SPEAKER_00]: Wholesome things means relaxation.

357
00:25:06,870 --> 00:25:11,153
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't know that, then the pleasure and kind of unwholesome is what relaxation is.

358
00:25:11,914 --> 00:25:14,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Distraction through sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

359
00:25:14,936 --> 00:25:16,798
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure through sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

360
00:25:19,760 --> 00:25:22,402
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you recognize that, no, that's not... that also requires effort.

361
00:25:24,232 --> 00:25:33,779
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the truly relaxing thing is the thing that is effortless, the thing that is free, the thing that doesn't require maintenance, the thing that doesn't require management, the thing that doesn't require fueling.

362
00:25:34,599 --> 00:25:35,720
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's not upādāna.

363
00:25:36,121 --> 00:25:38,362
[SPEAKER_00]: Upādāna is the fuel that requires the fueling.

364
00:25:39,723 --> 00:25:42,605
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, renunciation is a true relaxation.

365
00:25:43,246 --> 00:25:46,308
[SPEAKER_00]: Saying no to sensuality is a true way of relaxing.

366
00:25:47,758 --> 00:25:55,019
[SPEAKER_00]: But not for the one who still has the view that pertains to sensuality, that values sensuality, that doesn't see the danger in it.

367
00:25:56,660 --> 00:25:59,520
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, saying no to sensuality would mean, like, saying no to the pleasure of it.

368
00:25:59,600 --> 00:26:01,440
[SPEAKER_00]: And pleasure, for me, means relaxation.

369
00:26:02,021 --> 00:26:03,981
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, I am saying no to relaxation.

370
00:26:05,101 --> 00:26:15,623
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you have faith in renunciation, and remove your faith in the pleasure of senses, which is what you think relaxation is, you will come to see that, yeah, you were mistaken.

371
00:26:19,217 --> 00:26:21,137
[SPEAKER_00]: The true effortless thing is renunciation.

372
00:26:21,757 --> 00:26:22,398
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a negative.

373
00:26:23,238 --> 00:26:24,578
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not about doing this or that.

374
00:26:24,618 --> 00:26:26,658
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about not doing this or that.

375
00:26:27,739 --> 00:26:28,479
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's effortless.

376
00:26:32,239 --> 00:26:37,040
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's a freedom from unwholesome states, unwholesome qualities.

377
00:26:37,900 --> 00:26:40,581
[SPEAKER_01]: That's effortless.

378
00:26:41,021 --> 00:26:42,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure.

379
00:26:42,701 --> 00:26:45,242
[SPEAKER_01]: It requires knowledge of those things.

380
00:26:45,642 --> 00:26:46,722
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the...

381
00:26:48,110 --> 00:26:54,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Initial discernment, they can kind of allow you to... to get a sense of the direction of the practice.

382
00:26:56,297 --> 00:27:06,185
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you just stick with, I have faith in this or that, but you don't scrutinize it further, you don't reflect upon it further, well, then itself won't take you anywhere.

383
00:27:07,145 --> 00:27:11,909
[SPEAKER_01]: And without faith, you wouldn't... you wouldn't be able to interchange... well, cultivate jhāna.

384
00:27:11,949 --> 00:27:13,470
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, sure, sure, sure.

385
00:27:14,031 --> 00:27:14,892
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't get closer.

386
00:27:14,932 --> 00:27:15,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

387
00:27:19,137 --> 00:27:19,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly, yeah.

388
00:27:20,478 --> 00:27:21,459
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you wouldn't know where to look.

389
00:27:21,839 --> 00:27:22,300
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what I mean.

390
00:27:22,640 --> 00:27:26,624
[SPEAKER_00]: The right faith, it's like borrowing of that which you want to achieve.

391
00:27:28,385 --> 00:27:38,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, it can be very generic, inaccurate, just borrowing something without much, without concentrated substance in it, substance that you need.

392
00:27:40,376 --> 00:27:46,101
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if you scrutinize it, clarify it further, then your faith becomes more accurate as well.

393
00:27:47,017 --> 00:27:52,120
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, your faith actually pertains, in a more concentrated manner, to that which you're trying to achieve.

394
00:27:52,240 --> 00:27:52,440
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

395
00:27:52,460 --> 00:27:53,580
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you scrutinized it.

396
00:27:55,441 --> 00:27:59,863
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where the sadhana-sādhi, the one who kind of puts the faith first, has the weakest of the faculties.

397
00:28:04,806 --> 00:28:09,949
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he just, he just takes the faith, and does what he discerned through that faith.

398
00:28:10,989 --> 00:28:12,330
[SPEAKER_00]: Which, in scope, it's much...

399
00:28:14,412 --> 00:28:18,735
[SPEAKER_00]: More limited than for somebody who actually discerns even the faith.

400
00:28:18,935 --> 00:28:22,397
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he puts the discernment factor first, the dhammanusārī.

401
00:28:26,060 --> 00:28:30,062
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, dhammanusārī will carry his own faith through his discernment.

402
00:28:30,082 --> 00:28:33,464
[SPEAKER_00]: Sādhanusārī will be developing his discernment through his own faith.

403
00:28:34,325 --> 00:28:41,910
[SPEAKER_00]: But, yeah, if he put faith first and leave it first, it's much slower and more riskier today.

404
00:28:42,889 --> 00:28:46,571
[SPEAKER_00]: You could get away with that back in the day, when there were lots of arahants around you, teaching and so on.

405
00:28:47,712 --> 00:28:49,833
[SPEAKER_00]: But today, it's more like the other way.

406
00:28:50,674 --> 00:28:52,715
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, stuff you hear has nothing to do with the dhamma.

407
00:28:54,336 --> 00:29:00,540
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to first make the effort to undo all of the wrong notions of what you think dhamma is, and then start practicing right.

408
00:29:01,581 --> 00:29:06,604
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can't afford to just run off faith, because sooner or later you're going to learn that something's completely wrong.

409
00:29:06,984 --> 00:29:09,646
[SPEAKER_00]: And you won't see it, because you don't have a reference point at that stage.

410
00:29:15,777 --> 00:29:18,898
[SPEAKER_00]: But, putting discernment first, it's much more unpleasant.

411
00:29:19,939 --> 00:29:23,580
[SPEAKER_00]: More anxiety, more dread, more doubt, more pain.

412
00:29:24,521 --> 00:29:26,582
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, what's the big deal?

413
00:29:27,862 --> 00:29:30,964
[SPEAKER_00]: You suffered millions of lifetimes, you will suffer millions of lifetimes.

414
00:29:31,727 --> 00:29:32,808
[SPEAKER_00]: What is one lifetime more?

415
00:29:33,189 --> 00:29:35,231
[SPEAKER_00]: Because at least you're trying to practice dhamma, right?

416
00:29:35,431 --> 00:29:43,120
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, even if you suffer, as the Buddha said, even if you spend the rest of your life in tears of restraint and don't get anywhere, it still worth something.

417
00:29:43,541 --> 00:29:45,062
[SPEAKER_00]: It's still better, because you never do that.

418
00:29:46,624 --> 00:29:47,465
[SPEAKER_00]: For years and years,

419
00:29:55,862 --> 00:29:59,464
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't... if you can't figure it all out, it was confusing.

420
00:30:00,205 --> 00:30:02,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Faith in cultivating wholesome qualities.

421
00:30:02,746 --> 00:30:03,066
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

422
00:30:04,187 --> 00:30:06,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith in... Come back to the fundamentals.

423
00:30:07,429 --> 00:30:08,930
[SPEAKER_00]: It's easy to understand.

424
00:30:09,030 --> 00:30:09,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

425
00:30:10,350 --> 00:30:16,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that's why we've been speaking in such a... like a... well, in a fundamental manner.

426
00:30:16,134 --> 00:30:17,154
[SPEAKER_00]: It wouldn't be particular.

427
00:30:17,715 --> 00:30:18,975
[SPEAKER_00]: We have faith in this or that.

428
00:30:20,376 --> 00:30:21,977
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith in recognition of wholesome.

429
00:30:24,357 --> 00:30:28,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, if I stick to this, it can only result in good, which is why it's wholesome.

430
00:30:29,098 --> 00:30:33,039
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't see it directly, but I have a good faith that it is so.

431
00:30:38,901 --> 00:30:41,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and the same by body and speech and the mind.

432
00:30:41,522 --> 00:30:42,182
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not just...

433
00:30:43,735 --> 00:30:48,160
[SPEAKER_00]: You abstain from killing and lying and so on, but you also abstain from unwholesome speech.

434
00:30:48,240 --> 00:30:53,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So, gossip, careless chatter, all these things are unwholesome from their point of view.

435
00:30:53,866 --> 00:30:54,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you abstain from that.

436
00:30:56,809 --> 00:31:03,416
[SPEAKER_00]: You abstain from malicious speech, false speech, divisive speech.

437
00:31:04,697 --> 00:31:05,378
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you go further.

438
00:31:05,398 --> 00:31:06,419
[SPEAKER_00]: You abstain from thoughts.

439
00:31:08,018 --> 00:31:16,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you know you're not going to say anything or do anything, but you stop entertaining thoughts as well, of such nature.

440
00:31:19,048 --> 00:31:19,869
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the method.

441
00:31:20,369 --> 00:31:22,371
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the meditation technique you should be developing.

